  <h1>Consultation booking for architects</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Your office is open nine to five. Your next client is going to book a consultation at
    10:40 on a Sunday night, from the couch, and expect a slot on Monday. We know because we
    looked at twelve months of consultation calendars, and that is a very normal
    booking.
  </p>

  <p>
    We run client acquisition for architecture firms and the booked consultations land in calendars we can read. Between 14 October 2025 and 13 October 2026, 25 US architecture and design firms took 1,194 consultation bookings through those calendars. 838 of them were booked by the prospect personally, through the online booking page, which means the timestamp is the moment the client decided to act. Every figure below comes from that set.
  </p>

  <div class="answer">
    <p class="answer-label">The short answer</p>
    <p>Most architecture clients book outside office hours and want to meet soon. Across 838 self-booked consultations at 25 US firms, 74 percent were booked outside Monday to Friday, 9 to 5, and 29 percent on a weekend. 41 percent of all consultations took place within 24 hours of booking and three in four within three days. So give clients a calendar they can book at midnight, keep slots open in the next 48 hours, and protect Monday morning.</p>
  </div>

  <p>
    This page sits one step after <a href="/lead-generation-for-architects">lead generation for architects</a>. Getting the inquiry is the expensive part. Turning it into a meeting is where a surprising share of that money leaks out, and most of the leak is a scheduling problem.
  </p>

  <h2>When clients actually book an architect consultation</h2>
  <p>
    Here is every self-booked consultation by the hour it was booked, in the firm's own time zone, across all seven days of the week.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Booked between</th><th>Share of 838 self-booked consultations</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>6 am and 9 am</td><td>11.9%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>9 am and noon</td><td>16.7%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Noon and 5 pm</td><td>23.3%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5 pm and 10 pm</td><td>23.7%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>10 pm and 6 am</td><td>24.3%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    One in four bookings happened between ten at night and six in the morning. Put the weekends in and 73.5 percent of all self-booked consultations were made when a normal architecture office is closed. Sunday was the second busiest booking day of the week with 131, behind only Tuesday at 146.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pattern holds firm by firm. Of the 16 firms with at least 15 self-booked consultations, the off-hours share ran from 56 percent at the low end to 95 percent at the high end, with a median of 74.
  </p>
  <p>
    It makes sense once you picture who is booking. The person planning a house or a building has a day job. Architecture is the thing they think about after the kids are asleep, or on a Sunday with a coffee and a lot they just bought. A firm that can only be reached by phone during the day is asking that person to remember to call on Tuesday. Some will. Plenty won't.
  </p>

  <h2>How far in advance clients book a consultation</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Time from booking to the meeting</th><th>Consultations</th><th>Share</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Under 24 hours</td><td>489</td><td>41.0%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1 to 3 days</td><td>425</td><td>35.6%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3 to 7 days</td><td>236</td><td>19.8%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>More than a week</td><td>44</td><td>3.7%</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The median gap between booking and meeting was about 35 hours. One consultation in five happened on the same calendar day it was booked, typically under three hours later, and one in ten inside three hours.
  </p>
  <p>
    The firm-level spread is wide. Across the 19 firms with at least 15 bookings, the median lead time ran from under a day at the fastest firms to just over three days at the slowest, and the share of meetings held within 24 hours ranged from 24 to 67 percent. A good part of that gap is likely how much near-term availability each firm opens up, which is a setting you control.
  </p>
  <p>
    A fair caveat on the long end: most booking pages only open a week or two ahead, so the small share beyond seven days is partly the calendar's limit and not purely the client's preference.
  </p>

  <h2>Monday is the busiest consultation day, and Friday is where the wait hides</h2>
  <p>
    Monday carried 291 of the 1,194 meetings, 24.4 percent, more than any other day. Only eight consultations in the whole year happened on a Saturday or Sunday, at three firms. Meanwhile 244 self-booked consultations were made on a weekend, and 54.5 percent of those landed on Monday.
  </p>
  <p>
    A consultation booked on a Thursday took place a median 18.5 hours later. One booked on a Friday waited 65.8 hours, and one booked on Saturday 56.4. A client who decides on Friday afternoon is sitting with that decision through a whole weekend, which is plenty of time to also book the firm down the road that offered Saturday morning.
  </p>
  <p>
    You don't have to work weekends to fix it. The cheaper fix is to make Monday morning consultation time, so the weekend bookers get the first slots of the week, and to keep a couple of late Friday or Monday early slots for the people who would otherwise wait three days.
  </p>

  <h2>Residential and commercial clients run on different clocks</h2>
  <p>
    Only some of the firms in the set are tagged by segment, so treat this as a direction and not a benchmark: seven residential firms with 382 bookings and three commercial firms with 228.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you do <strong>residential</strong> work, your clients book more on weekends (32 percent of self-booked consultations, against 24 percent for commercial) and wait a little longer, a median of about a day and a half from booking to meeting. A homeowner planning a build is usually doing it around a job and a family, and wants a meeting both partners can make. Evening and early slots carry more weight for you than for anyone.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you do <strong>commercial</strong> work, the client moves faster: 54 percent of commercial consultations happened within 24 hours, against 40 percent residential, and the median wait was under a day.
  </p>
  <p>
    A developer or business owner who books is often working against a closing date or a lease. They still booked outside office hours 76 percent of the time, so "commercial clients only call during business hours" does not hold up in our data. What they want is a same-day or next-day slot and an architect who arrives with a view on cost and schedule.
  </p>
  <p>
    Both buyers find you the same way, which is why <a href="/meta-ads-for-architects">Meta ads for architects</a> work for both segments, with different creative and different follow-up. The deeper playbooks are in <a href="/marketing-for-residential-architects">marketing for residential architects</a> and <a href="/marketing-for-commercial-architects">marketing for commercial architects</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>How to set up your consultation calendar</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Let clients book themselves, at any hour.</strong> Put a booking link in the thank-you message of every form, in the first automatic reply, and on your website's contact page (more on what that page needs in <a href="/website-design-for-architects">website design for architects</a>). 838 of the 1,194 bookings in our set came through that kind of link.</li>
    <li><strong>Keep slots open in the next 48 hours.</strong> 41 percent of meetings happened inside a day. If the earliest slot you show is next Thursday, a lot of those people simply book someone else.</li>
    <li><strong>Treat Monday morning as consultation time.</strong> That is where weekend bookers land, and a quarter of all meetings fell on Monday.</li>
    <li><strong>Close the Friday gap.</strong> One late Friday or early Monday slot cuts a three-day wait to a few hours for the people who book at the end of the week.</li>
    <li><strong>Send the confirmation and reminders automatically.</strong> A booking made at midnight should get a reply at midnight, not when the office opens.</li>
    <li><strong>Ask one or two qualifying questions on the booking page.</strong> Only about a quarter of bookings came with any note at all, and the typical note was twelve words long. If budget and project type matter to you, ask for them in the form.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    On point five, the cost of a slow reply has been measured outside our niche for a long time. A 2011 audit published in <a href="https://hbr.org/2011/03/the-short-life-of-online-sales-leads" rel="nofollow">Harvard Business Review</a> tested 2,241 US companies with a web inquiry and found that the ones who replied within 30 days took an average of 42 hours, and 23 percent never replied at all. Our own take on the first reply is in <a href="/email-marketing-for-architects#the-first-email-is-a-race-and-most-firms-lose-it">the first email is a race</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What this data can't tell you</h2>
  <p>
    Honest limits. The clock in every figure is the firm's calendar time zone, which matches the client for almost every local firm but not for every booking. The segment split rests on ten firms. And the calendars record very few outcomes.
  </p>
  <p>
    Just 6.7 percent of bookings are marked cancelled, with no clear pattern by how far ahead they were booked (7.4 percent under a day, 5.9 percent at one to three days, 8.1 percent at three to seven), and so few show or no-show results are filled in that we do not publish a show rate.
  </p>
  <p>
    What we can say is how many inquiries tend to become a meeting at all. In our <a href="/architecture-marketing-benchmark-report-2026#from-lead-to-booked-consultation">benchmark report on lead to booked consultation</a>, the median firm booked 32 percent of its leads, with the stronger quarter above 45 percent and the weaker quarter under 15. A big part of that spread sits in the habits on this page. If you want to see what a budget turns into at each of those rates, the <a href="/architecture-marketing-budget-calculator">architecture marketing budget calculator</a> runs it for you.
  </p>

  <h2>A few honest answers</h2>
  <h3>When do clients book consultations with architects?</h3>
  <p>
    Mostly when your office is closed. Across 838 consultations that clients booked themselves at 25 US architecture firms over twelve months, 74 percent were booked outside Monday to Friday, 9 to 5, 29 percent on a Saturday or Sunday, and about a quarter between 10 pm and 6 am. Sunday was the second busiest booking day of the week. If the only way to reach you is a phone call during working hours, you are missing the hours when most people actually decide.
  </p>
  <h3>How far in advance should an architect schedule a consultation?</h3>
  <p>
    As soon as the client wants, within reason. In our data 41 percent of consultations took place within 24 hours of booking, three in four within three days, and fewer than one in twenty more than a week out. The median gap was about 35 hours. Commercial clients moved faster than residential ones. Showing open slots in the next one to two days is the setting that matches how people book.
  </p>
  <h3>Should architects offer evening or weekend consultations?</h3>
  <p>
    You can get most of the benefit without it. Only eight of 1,194 consultations in our set happened on a weekend, while more than half of weekend bookings were simply taken for Monday. What matters is that a client can book on the weekend, gets an instant confirmation, and finds a slot early in the week. Residential firms, whose clients book on weekends more often, have the best case for an occasional evening slot.
  </p>

  <h2>Make it easy to say yes at midnight</h2>
  <p>
    Getting a serious client to raise their hand costs real money, and a firm that then makes them wait until Tuesday to talk is paying for leads it hands to someone else. A booking page, two days of open slots, and a protected Monday morning fix most of it. If you would rather have the whole thing run for you, from the ads to the booked consultation on your calendar, that is what we do for architecture firms.
  </p>
